.TH NETLAB 1 "2026-09-23" "netlab 2.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
netlab \- network analysis and interception workbench for Kali Linux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B netlab
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B NetLab
is a GUI-first network analyser and interception tool. It captures packets
with
.BR dumpcap (1)
into a real pcap or pcapng file and then decodes those bytes itself to show
live traffic, connections, hosts, DNS, cleartext HTTP and TLS handshake
metadata.
.PP
NetLab has two halves. The
.B passive
half never puts a frame on the wire: it observes, decodes and, when
credential extraction is enabled, recovers the passwords and authentication
hashes that readable protocols hand to anyone on the path.
.PP
The
.B active
half transmits: full\-duplex ARP poisoning, SSL stripping, TLS interception
with a locally generated certificate authority, DNS spoofing, a rogue DHCP
server, an in\-flight traffic changer, cookie expiry and a promiscuous\-host
probe.
.PP
Nothing in the active half runs without an
.IR engagement :
a named, on\-disk record of the interface, the gateway and the hosts the
operator declared to be in scope, confirmed in a dialog that states what is
about to be transmitted. Every address is checked against that engagement in
the privileged helper, and every action is appended to a JSONL audit log.
Active interception is for networks you have been authorised to test.
.PP
Protocol fields that were not observed on the wire are shown as absent rather
than guessed.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a help message and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interface " \fIIFACE\fR"
Preselect this capture interface.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-filter " \fIBPF\fR"
Preload a libpcap capture filter expression.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-read " \fIFILE\fR"
Open a pcap or pcapng file on startup instead of capturing.
.TP
.B \-\-start
Begin capturing immediately on the selected interface.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Report capture privileges and available interfaces on stdout, then exit.
This works without a graphical display.
.SH PRIVILEGES
NetLab is intended to run as an ordinary desktop user. Capture privileges
live in
.BR dumpcap (1),
which on Debian and Kali carries
.B cap_net_raw
and
.B cap_net_admin
and is executable by the
.B wireshark
group. To grant a user capture rights:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo dpkg\-reconfigure wireshark\-common
sudo usermod \-aG wireshark $USER
.fi
.RE
.PP
The group change takes effect at the next login. Run
.B netlab \-\-check
to confirm. Running the whole GUI as root works but is not recommended.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/netlab/netlab.conf
System-wide default settings, in JSON.
.TP
.I ~/.config/netlab/config.json
Per-user settings, overriding the system defaults.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/netlab/captures
Default directory for capture files.
.SH DISPLAY FILTERS
Each table has a filter box that narrows the rows already captured without
affecting the capture itself. Terms are combined with AND, a leading
.B \-
negates, and
.B |
separates alternatives:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ip:10.0.0.5      port:443       proto:tls|http
host:example.com \-proto:arp
.fi
.RE
.PP
To change what is captured in the first place, use the BPF capture filter in
the toolbar; see
.BR pcap-filter (7).
.SH PRIVILEGES
NetLab itself runs unprivileged. Capture rights live in
.BR dumpcap (1);
see
.B \-\-check.
.PP
Interception needs
.B CAP_NET_RAW
and
.BR CAP_NET_ADMIN ,
which live in a separate process,
.BR netlab-helper (1).
The GUI starts it through
.BR pkexec (1)
the first time an engagement is armed and drives it over a pipe. When the GUI
exits the pipe closes, and the helper restores ARP tables, packet forwarding
and its nftables table before exiting, so a crashed GUI cannot leave a subnet
poisoned.
.PP
Run
.B netlab-helper \-\-check
as root to report whether the interception prerequisites are present.
.SH ENGAGEMENT FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/netlab/engagements/<date>-<name>/engagement.json
The declared scope: interface, gateway, targets and the operator's
confirmation.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/netlab/engagements/<date>-<name>/audit.jsonl
Every active action, one JSON object per line.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/netlab/engagements/<date>-<name>/files/
Response bodies carved while file capture was enabled.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/netlab/ca/
The interception certificate authority and the per\-name leaves minted from
it. Install
.I ca.crt
on the device under test to intercept its TLS without a warning, and remove
it when the engagement ends.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Normal termination.
.TP
.B 1
.B \-\-check
found that capture is not currently possible.
.TP
.B 2
No graphical display was available.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dumpcap (1),
.BR tshark (1),
.BR wireshark (1),
.BR capinfos (1),
.BR pcap-filter (7),
.BR nmap (1),
.BR nft (8),
.BR pkexec (1),
.BR hashcat (1)
